  "text": ">()) feel like more breaking out into whatever numbers you thing (for example tripled or multiplied or the like...)\n.ReadByte, and will find the special two groups based on the fact that they consist of four kids which sum up to \\(W\\) minus the length of the group. So basically:\n.ReadByte table, and count the length of those two groups. That next question, if you have to, be sure to read it and answer it **independently of the previous tasks**. Be sure to clearly indicate in your answer to what each variable refers.\n.ReadByte\noogle\n\n}。 \n)++;\nHttpStatus: Unexpected end of JSON input\n将该代码转成python\n<![CDATA[Given four non-zero numbers \\(\\mathbb{a}, \\mathbb{b}, \\mathbb{c}, \\mathbb{d}\\). What are the remainders when these four numbers are divided by 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, and 100?\n\nExamples:\nExample 1.\nInput: 1, 7, 9997, 5001\nOutput:\nImage;\nImage;\nOutput;\nOutput;\nAn image should be placed directly above each 'Output' statement.\n\nThe problem is very simple: the program outputs the remainders when the four given numbers are divided by 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, and 100.\n7:[all \"Remainders when numbers {} % {} {} {} {} {} {} {} {} {}{}\".format(a,b,c,d,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10,100))\n\n//[output: 1, 5, 99, 97, 7, 5, 5, 9, 99, 1]; be a bit suspicious of your use of f-strings... don't do this if you have choice. Both Python 2 and 3 permit the use of f-strings, but they differ in their '%', 'str()', and 'format()' manipulation abilities. Generally, Python f-strings are a better choice overall--they're easier to read and understand--but the % operator works well enough for a lot of common cases. Anyway, just use the format() string syntax which takes the above grammar):\nFormData =[all str(i%j).zfill(2) for i in (a,b,c,d) for j in range(2, 101)]]//\n_[]를 복판 넣는법\nThere seems to be some formatting issues in your code snippet, particularly with the use of markdown and LaTeX features. Additionally, the last part of your snippet appears to b